Security controls can be classified not only by their category (managerial, operational, technical, physical) but also by their functional type, which refers to the specific goal or purpose the control serves in protecting systems and data. Here's a breakdown of the key functional types:

🔐 Functional Types of Security Controls
1. 	Preventive Controls
• 	Purpose: Stop threats before they occur.
• 	Timing: Operate before an attack.
• 	Examples:
• 	Access control lists (ACLs) on firewalls
• 	Antimalware software that blocks malicious code
• 	Security policies that restrict unauthorized actions
2. 	Detective Controls
• 	Purpose: Identify and record security incidents.
• 	Timing: Operate during an attack or unauthorized activity.
• 	Examples:
• 	System and network logs
• 	Intrusion detection systems (IDS)
• 	Security cameras and monitoring tools
3. 	Corrective Controls
• 	Purpose: Minimize damage and restore systems after an incident.
• 	Timing: Operate after an attack.
• 	Examples:
• 	Backup and recovery systems
• 	Patch management tools
• 	Reimaging compromised systems
4. 	Directive Controls
• 	Purpose: Guide behavior and enforce rules.
• 	Timing: Ongoing, often embedded in organizational culture.
• 	Examples:
• 	Security policies and SOPs
• 	Employee contracts with disciplinary clauses
• 	Training and awareness programs
5. 	Deterrent Controls
• 	Purpose: Discourage malicious actions through psychological or legal pressure.
• 	Timing: Before or during an attempted intrusion.
• 	Examples:
• 	Warning signs about surveillance or legal consequences
• 	Visible security measures like guards or cameras
• 	Publicized enforcement of security policies
6. 	Compensating Controls
• 	Purpose: Provide alternative protection when primary controls are not feasible.
• 	Timing: Can be preventive, detective, or corrective depending on implementation.
• 	Examples:
• 	Network segmentation to isolate vulnerable systems
• 	Manual review processes when automated controls are unavailable
• 	Enhanced monitoring for legacy systems lacking modern security features

🧠 Summary of Timing and Purpose
• 	Preventive: Before → Block threats
• 	Detective: During → Identify threats
• 	Corrective: After → Recover from threats
• 	Directive: Always → Guide behavior
• 	Deterrent: Before/During → Discourage threats
• 	Compensating: Flexible → Substitute for missing controls
